## Solution

We claim the only complete set is $\mathbb{R}$ itself.

### A key consequence of completeness

Suppose $A$ is complete and $s\in A$. For an arbitrary real $a$, set $b=s-a$. Then $a+b=s\in A$, so by completeness $ab=a(s-a)\in A$. As $a$ ranges over $\mathbb{R}$,

$$a(s-a)=-\Bigl(a-\tfrac{s}{2}\Bigr)^{2}+\tfrac{s^{2}}{4}$$

ranges over the whole interval $\bigl(-\infty,\,\tfrac{s^{2}}{4}\bigr]$. Hence

$$s\in A \;\Longrightarrow\; \bigl(-\infty,\,\tfrac{s^{2}}{4}\bigr]\subseteq A. \tag{$\ast$}$$

### Forcing $0\in A$, then $(-\infty,0]\subseteq A$

Since $A\neq\varnothing$, pick $s\in A$. By $(\ast)$, $\bigl(-\infty,\tfrac{s^{2}}{4}\bigr]\subseteq A$; in particular $0\in A$ (as $0\le s^{2}/4$). Applying $(\ast)$ with $s=0$ gives

$$(-\infty,0]\subseteq A. \tag{1}$$

### Forcing $1\in A$, then $(-\infty,\tfrac14]\subseteq A$

Using (1), $-2\in A$. Take $a=b=-1$: $a+b=-2\in A$, so completeness gives $ab=1\in A$. Applying $(\ast)$ with $s=1$:

$$\bigl(-\infty,\tfrac14\bigr]\subseteq A. \tag{2}$$

### Forcing all of $[0,\infty)\subseteq A$

Let $t>0$ be arbitrary and set $a=b=-t$. Then $a+b=-2t\le \tfrac14$, so $-2t\in A$ by (2). Completeness yields $ab=t^{2}\in A$. Since $t>0$ was arbitrary and $t^{2}$ ranges over all of $(0,\infty)$, we get

$$(0,\infty)\subseteq A. \tag{3}$$

### Conclusion

From (2) and (3),

$$A\;\supseteq\;\bigl(-\infty,\tfrac14\bigr]\;\cup\;(0,\infty)\;=\;\mathbb{R},$$

so $A=\mathbb{R}$.

Conversely, $\mathbb{R}$ is trivially complete: $a+b\in\mathbb{R}$ and $ab\in\mathbb{R}$ for all real $a,b$.

Therefore the unique complete set of real numbers is

$$\boxed{A=\mathbb{R}}.$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
